fix: grant the window permissions the title bar actually needs

The window could not be dragged and the close button did nothing, which turned
out to be one cause, not two.

`core:default` only grants the window plugin's *getters* — `allow-is-maximized`,
`allow-inner-size`, `allow-theme` and friends. It does **not** grant
`start_dragging`, `close`, `minimize`, `maximize`, `unmaximize` or
`toggle-maximize`. So every one of those calls was rejected by the ACL, and
because the frontend discarded the returned promise the failure was completely
invisible: the window simply never moved and the close button did nothing.

The capability now lists the window permissions dshell actually uses, with a
comment explaining that `core:default` is not enough for any of them.

Two things so this cannot come back silently:

  * `web/ipc.ts` wraps each window operation and reports the error instead of
    swallowing it, and the strip surfaces that as a visible toast. A silently
    rejected invoke is the real trap here, not the missing permission.
  * the strip no longer relies on a full-bleed drag overlay sitting under every
    child in z-order. `data-tauri-drag-region` with a bare value only drags when
    the click target is *exactly* that element, which one stray child element
    defeats; the strip is now `="deep"` and its interactive zones opt out with
    `="false"`, so the draggable area is the whole strip minus the things that
    need clicks.

Verified by rebuilding and checking that the compiled
`src-tauri/gen/schemas/capabilities.json` contains the granted permissions,
rather than assuming the JSON took effect.
